      "content": "Referring to your suggestion, it's also essential to check the originating IP addresses allowed by S3 bucket policies. Sometimes, trying to access an S3 bucket from a place outside your AWS VPC, e.g., over the internet, or a different AWS region can result in access denials. You can either allow public access for debugging purposes or restrict access to known IP ranges. Here's how you can alter your bucket policy to allow access from certain IP addresses:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Sid\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Principal\": {\n        \"Service\": \"lambda.amazonaws.com\"\n      },\n      \"Action\": \"sts:AssumeRole\",\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:iam::<account-id>:role/<lambda-function-role>\",\n      \"Condition\": {\n        \"StringEquals\": {\n          \"aws:SourceArn\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-company-logs\"\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"Sid\": \"Deny\",\n      \"Effect\": \"Deny\",\n      \"Principal\": \"*\",\n      \"Action\": \"s3:PutObject\",\n      \"Resource",
